he program to Australia, including industrial costs and benefits received and forecast; (c) changes in the acquisition timeline; (d) the performance of the aircraft in testing; (e) potential alternatives to the Joint Strike Fighter; and (f) any other related matters. Stephen Conroy I seek leave to speak for a minute on this motion. Leave granted. While Labor strongly support the right of the Senate to inquire into a whole range of issues, we do not want the fact that Labor are supporting this Senate inquiry to remotely suggest that Labor do not fully support Australia's participation in the F35 project. We are strongly behind it, we have a long record of being strongly behind it and we continue to support Australia's participation in this project. Scott Ryan I seek leave to make a short statement. Stephen Parry Leave is granted for one minute. Scott Ryan The government does not support this motion, and, despite what Senator Conroy just stated to the chamber, the fact that the opposition is doing so is embarrassing. Successive Australian governments have been committed to the JSF program, including in Labor's 2009 and 2013 white papers. Stephen Conroy We continue to be. Stephen Parry Order on my left! Scott Ryan Both the Leader of the Opposition and the shadow minister, Senator Conroy, have made strong statements supporting the JSF. To date, Australian industry has secured contracts valued at US$448 million. We estimate that Australian industry will win at least US$1.5 billion in JSF related orders. This creates jobs. Last week the UK confirm","result":"affirmative","slug":"division-federal-senate-4028","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2015-12-02/3"}
